{%- comment -%}
  Wear Label Custom — the B2B hook on the home page. Brief §7.

  This band is the whole reason the brief exists. Wear Label has always done two
  things — ready-to-wear for shoppers, custom apparel production for companies,
  hospitals, schools and communities — and the site has only ever said the first.
  A visitor who runs an organisation had no way to learn the second business
  exists, so this band's single job is to tell them, on the home page, above the
  fold of the second screenful.

  WHY IT IS CREAM AND NOT ESPRESSO. Espresso is the loudest surface this theme
  has, and "make the B2B band noticeable" argues for it — but the voices wall
  directly below is already espresso, and two espresso bands touching read as one
  very long dark region with a seam in it. Cream is the design's declared band
  colour and it is the tonal step the service band already uses for the same
  reason. The band gets its weight from the aurora, the photograph and the pair
  of buttons instead of from the darkest paint available.

  THE AURORA CONTRACT, restated because breaking it is subtle: this band carries
  `relative isolate overflow-hidden` and the aurora's `tone` is `canvas` because
  the band is `bg-canvas`. The veil layer is painted in the colour of the surface
  underneath, so a tone that does not match the band draws a visible rectangle —
  that is the bug this effect always has. `isolate` is what keeps `soft-light`
  blending against the band rather than against the page.

  AND NOTHING HERE IS WRAPPED IN [data-reveal] THAT CONTAINS THE AURORA. The
  reveals animate opacity, which makes their element a stacking context, which
  would flatten the blend against the wrapper instead of the band. The two
  `data-reveal` divs below are the aurora's SIBLINGS, never its ancestors. If you
  add a wrapper here, check that rule again before you add it.

  THE PHOTOGRAPH IS FIRST IN THE DOM and moves to the right column at `lg`. Two
  reasons, and the second is why the reading-order mismatch is acceptable: the
  brief's design direction leads with large photography and a phone should open
  this band on an image rather than on a heading, and the image is a labelled
  `media` placeholder carrying no information a reader loses by meeting it in a
  different order than a sighted reader does. There is no B2B photography in the
  repo yet, so today this renders the placeholder at its final size — which is
  the state the studio asked for until they send project shots.

  Service names are blocks, not a hardcoded list, because the three the brief
  names (Custom Uniform, Corporate Apparel, Merchandise & Community Apparel) are
  the services as they stand today and the studio adds a fourth without a code
  change. They are labels here, not descriptions — the descriptions are on the
  B2B page, and repeating them would make this band a second version of that page
  rather than a route to it.
{%- endcomment -%}

<section
  aria-labelledby="custom-band-heading"
  class="relative isolate mt-section overflow-hidden border-y border-rule bg-canvas"
>
  {% render 'aurora', tone: 'canvas', origin: 'top-right' %}

  <div class="relative z-10 mx-auto grid w-full max-w-content gap-block px-gutter py-section lg:grid-cols-2 lg:items-center lg:gap-block-lg">
    <div data-reveal class="lg:order-last">
      {% render 'media',
        image: section.settings.image,
        ratio: '4 / 3',
        sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 46vw, 92vw',
        label: 'Custom apparel project photo' %}
    </div>

    <div data-reveal class="flex flex-col gap-5">
      <span class="text-micro uppercase tracking-eyebrow text-ink-subtle">
        {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.eyebrow, label: 'eyebrow', inline: true %}
      </span>

      <h2 id="custom-band-heading" class="text-h1 leading-h1">
        {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.heading, label: 'heading' %}
      </h2>

      <p class="text-h3 leading-h3 text-ink-body">
        {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.subheading, label: 'subheading', lines: 2 %}
      </p>

      <p class="wl-measure text-body leading-body text-ink-muted">
        {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.body, label: 'description', lines: 3 %}
      </p>

      {%- if section.blocks.size > 0 -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A rule per service rather than chips: three chips of unequal length
          wrap into a ragged two-and-one on a phone, and these are a list of what
          the studio makes, not a set of filters to choose between.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <ul class="flex flex-col divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
          {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
            <li class="py-3.5 text-caption uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-body" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
              {% render 'copy', value: block.settings.label, label: 'service', inline: true %}
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if section.settings.cta != blank or section.settings.cta_2 != blank -%}
        <div class="mt-1 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          {%- if section.settings.cta != blank -%}
            {% render 'button',
              label: section.settings.cta,
              href: section.settings.href,
              variant: 'primary',
              class: 'max-sm:w-full px-6 sm:px-11' %}
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if section.settings.cta_2 != blank -%}
            {% render 'button',
              label: section.settings.cta_2,
              href: section.settings.href_2,
              variant: 'secondary',
              class: 'max-sm:w-full px-6 sm:px-11' %}
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Custom & business band",
  "max_blocks": 4,
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The B2B route on the home page. Keep the service names here to labels — the descriptions belong on the Custom & Business page, and repeating them here makes this band a copy of that page rather than a way into it." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "Custom apparel" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Headline", "default": "Wear Label Custom" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "subheading", "label": "Subheadline", "default": "Custom apparel for companies, organizations & communities." },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Description", "default": "From uniforms to merchandise, Wear Label provides custom apparel production tailored to your team, organization, and brand." },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Photograph", "info": "A photograph of work the studio has actually produced. Leave it empty and a labelled placeholder renders at the final size rather than a stock image standing in for a project." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Buttons" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta", "label": "Primary label", "default": "Explore custom services" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "href", "label": "Primary link" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta_2", "label": "Secondary label", "default": "Request a quote" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "href_2", "label": "Secondary link" }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "service",
      "name": "Service name",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "text", "id": "label", "label": "Name" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Custom & business band",
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "service", "settings": { "label": "Custom uniform" } },
        { "type": "service", "settings": { "label": "Corporate apparel" } },
        { "type": "service", "settings": { "label": "Merchandise & community apparel" } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
